openlinkhub (0.9.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The edition's build of OpenLinkHub 0.9.4 (Nikola Jurkovic, github.com/jurkovic-nikola/OpenLinkHub, release of 2026-11-01), carried because Debian never packaged it (no source package in Debian, no removal record in ftp-master's logs read 2018 to 2026: nothing against it) and SparkyLinux does not host it. It is the program that owns an iCUE LINK hub, power supply or cooler where OpenRGB and OpenLinkHub would otherwise read the same device (sparky-stereo-hardware 0.1.30 and later).
  * Built from the tag's source, pinned in the recipe by commit a08204cf88fd373a0bbdbe1f4382e8c64024adca, the tarball's SHA-256 and the git tree id 5e14fe011058ab371880bad1699f2fda784f2625, with its seven Go modules vendored (go.mod and go.sum unchanged: hashes checked before and after `go mod vendor`), GOTOOLCHAIN=local, GOPROXY=off, -mod=vendor, -trimpath, GOAMD64=v1. The program is not changed. Upstream's prebuilt program and .deb are not used.
  * The filesystem hierarchy instead of /opt: the program in /usr/bin, the page's files and default database in /usr/share/openlinkhub, its working folder and state in /var/lib/openlinkhub (it reads and writes everything relative to its working folder), the system user openlinkhub from sysusers.d, upstream's udev rules in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d. openlinkhub-seed links web/ and static/ and seeds database/ without ever overwriting a file the person or the program changed.
  * The service is enabled and started when the package is installed, as upstream's installer does; a person's own disable is kept across upgrades. A copy installed with upstream's scripts (/opt/OpenLinkHub, or its unit in /etc/systemd/system) is left alone: this package then enables, starts, restarts and stops nothing, moves no profile, and tells the person once, at the install, how to change over (README.Debian).
  * Lean Code: the language plan names c (the package's own src/audio C code); the Go files have no adapter yet and stay authored, and vendor/ is preserved (Go assembler headers and hidapi's C files are not C for the toolkit), as for edgetk. The licences of the seven vendored modules, from their own files, are in debian/copyright (generated; the build stops when it differs).

 -- Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0300
